264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326911 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134074 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186244257 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["activity-9825eb55-72d5-5b07-8e8a-c069c488575b" :activity/id #uuid "9825eb55-72d5-5b07-8e8a-c069c488575b"] ["step-0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" :step/id #uuid "0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f"] ["step-fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296" :step/id #uuid "fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296"] ["step-f22dc590-f6dc-5c55-9547-5f97dd757472" :step/id #uuid "f22dc590-f6dc-5c55-9547-5f97dd757472"] ["step-a6ba7b18-0939-5bd9-b0f6-82c7785070e6" :step/id #uuid "a6ba7b18-0939-5bd9-b0f6-82c7785070e6"] ["step-8016382a-6b8c-54d4-9839-9c63e2a7f47d" :step/id #uuid "8016382a-6b8c-54d4-9839-9c63e2a7f47d"] ["step-0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" :step/content 17592186326906] ["step-0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" :step/next "step-fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296"] ["step-fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296" :step/content 17592186134367] ["step-f